  "result": "The premise is false. More agents do not always give better results.\n\n**Why it matters:** your budget is small. Each agent makes its own model calls. Agents also pass messages to each other. Each hand-off costs tokens and can lose detail.\n\n**Example:** you ask a friend to bring milk. You tell a second friend to tell a third friend. The third friend brings oat drink. More people in the chain gave more chances for error.\n\n**When splitting helps:**\n- The tasks are truly separate.\n- One agent's context gets too big.\n- Parts can run at the same time.\n\n**When it does not help:** your assistant has three jobs: answer from manuals, check the calendar, and book time. These jobs depend on each other. A booking needs the machine rules first. One agent with three tools is probably enough. Five or ten agents would cost more and add more hand-offs.\n\nSo the right number is the smallest one that works. Start with one. Split only when you can name a problem the split fixes.\n\nI can teach a lesson on when multi-agent systems pay off, if you want.\n\n**Question:** a student asks, \"Can I use the laser cutter on Friday?\" Which of your three jobs does the assistant need, and in what order?",
